 "result": "Both methods do the same thing, and the chain is:\n\n1. **`send_static_file`** (`src/tessel/app.py:390`, `src/tessel/bundles.py:82`) calls `self.static_cache_seconds(filename)` and passes the result as `max_age` to `send_from_directory`.\n2. **`static_cache_seconds`** (`app.py:363`, `bundles.py:55`) reads the `STATIC_CACHE_SECONDS_DEFAULT` config value:\n   - `None` (the default since 2.0) sends no timed cache. Browsers revalidate with conditional requests.\n   - An int or `timedelta` becomes `max_age` in seconds.\n3. **`src/tessel/helpers.py:406`** is the fallback in `send_file`. It uses `ctx.app.static_cache_seconds` when no `max_age` is passed. See also the docs at `helpers.py:515` and `docs/config.rst:255`.\n\n**Where to check for your stale CSS:**\n- Look at the app's config for `STATIC_CACHE_SECONDS_DEFAULT`. If it's set to a large value, that is why browsers keep the old file.\n- Look for a subclass of `Tessel` or `Bundle` that overrides `static_cache_seconds`. Tests do this at `tests/test_bundles.py:223`.\n- Check for a CDN or reverse proxy in front of the app that adds its own caching headers.\n- Note that `bundles.py:72` uses `active_app.config`, not the bundle's own config. A bundle follows the active app's setting, so bundle-specific config won't change it.\n\nIf the setting is `None` and files are still stale, the cause is outside this code. The usual fixes there are a fingerprinted filename or a `?v=` query string.",
